    "result_text": "The full suite is green (only `AcceptanceTest` exists, containing the new `coreCountsNamesakes` test).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same firstName and lastName, case-insensitively \u2014 on the `POST /api/owners` OfficeFloor pipeline.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **New function step** `AssignNamesakeCount.java` \u2014 counts existing owners (via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, excluding the not-yet-saved new owner) whose first and last names match case-insensitively, and stores the result on the owner. Placed after `AssignCustomerCode` and before `SaveOwner`.\n\n2. **Wired it into** `owners.POST.yml` with `govern: [ transaction ]`, matching the surrounding steps.\n\n3. **Owner entity** (`Owner.java`) \u2014 added a persisted `Integer namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct auto-maps it into the response by name.\n\n4. **API schema** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema so it's serialized in the `GET`/`POST` responses.\n\n5. **DDL** \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres); tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in the SQL schema.\n\nThe value is computed once at creation and persisted, so it's returned unchanged by later `GET /api/owners/{id}` reads \u2014 which is exactly what the acceptance test asserts (`namesakeCount == 1` for the second owner sharing name with the first).",
